{"Max-count cleanup can loop forever after a no-op batch because the callback re-fetches the same LIMIT/OFFSET window on every iteration and never changes the offset, causing fetchIDs to keep returning the same non-empty ID list without making progress": 0.31570032238960266, "SQLite fallback in deleteByIDs switches to literal SQL only when AnnotationCleanupJobBatchSize > 999, but the SQLite failure mode is driven by the number of IDs in the current batch (len(ids)), not the configured batch size, so batches longer than 999 will still fail with too many placeholders": 0.7957410216331482, "Orphaned tag cleanup is skipped when no annotations were deleted because the refactor gates tag cleanup on totalCleanedAnnotations > 0, leaving stale annotation_tag rows behind indefinitely when the job is otherwise a no-op": 0.05774889141321182, "Batch insert test helper createTestAnnotations ignores InsertMulti errors from each chunk because it only calls require.NoError inside the closure but returns nil unconditionally, potentially leaving the database in a partially populated state on partial insert failure": 0.7097223997116089, "Single-delete code path in the Delete method still uses subqueries (DELETE FROM annotation_tag WHERE annotation_id IN (SELECT id FROM annotation ...)) and was not converted to the new fetch-then-delete pattern, leaving it vulnerable to cross-database DELETE semantic issues": 0.18776969611644745}